  "levels_won_this_session": [],
  "novel_mechanics_found": "Level 9's sockets can capture a fruit strongly enough that later pulls at nearby coordinates stop moving it, so socketing c before the hostile fusion is complete can strand the remaining hunters. Hunter composites can be fully occluded by a fruit: in one rendered transition three of four visible 7 hunters vanished while c and one 7 remained, with no separately visible e/d pixels. Moving the socketed f deeper inside its socket advanced the hostile collision and immediately changed c into a b, confirming that socket overlap is not protection. The bottom resource meter is not one cell per env.step: failed or long-distance clicks can consume multiple cells, and ACTION7 restores the prior board transition but not that resource.",
  "narrative": "The 145-action banked prefix was confirmed from a fresh reset before exploration. Three forward Level 9 boards were investigated using only rendered grids and action outcomes. The strongest line merged the two 6 pieces into f, socketed f, converted 8 into c, and collapsed three of the four hunters in one transition while preserving both required fruits; the final visible 7 could not be merged before the resource meter expired. No Level 9 win was observed, so the maximum remains 8. A final fresh-reset reproduce() gate replayed the 145-action sequence to levels_completed=8 and passed; total usage was 775 env.step calls.",
  "gotchas": [
    "Level 9 requires c, f, and d for three 9 sockets; producing/socketing c and f alone does not win.",
    "Two 6 blocks merge into f. A hunter contact can degrade f back to 6 and then to a; socket overlap does not make a fruit safe.",
    "Two 7 hunters overlapping form a nine-pixel e. The observed e/fruit contact converted 8 into c; a later contact degraded c into b.",
    "The lower-left hunter's default route toward the 8 runs through the two starting 6 resources after about five actions, so those resources must be moved or merged immediately.",
    "Moving the first 6 toward (26,46) catches both starting 6 pieces in the pull radius and merges them immediately into f; this unexpectedly useful right-staging route preserved f while 8 became c.",
    "On the best board, c could be kited from about (37,17) along (29,17),(21,17),(13,21),(6,25),(6,33),(6,40), but the predators remained active and the fruit was not permanently safe on a socket.",
    "ACTION7 rewinds one rendered board step without advancing hunters, but the bottom resource meter remains consumed; repeated rewinds are not free.",
    "Clicks near two different fruit tiers can fail from attraction-radius interference; this made simultaneous c/f juggling especially tight.",
    "A fruit placed well inside a 9 socket may stop responding to outward pulls; do not fully socket c until the d fusion is settled.",
    "A composite hunter can be completely hidden underneath c; absence of visible e/d-colored pixels does not prove the fusion failed.",
    "The bottom resource meter can drain by more than one cell per env.step, including on failed coordinate pulls, so env-step budget and in-game move budget must be tracked separately.",
    "A useful observed partial line after the prefix was (26,46),(20,49),(14,53),(28,48),(22,44): it sockets f, produces c, and clusters the hunters, but still needs a safe final d-fusion and delivery continuation.",
    "At the final level, a new env.reset() is not a cheap current-level retry in prior provenance, so each clean attempt required replaying the full 145-action prefix."
  ],
  "solve_provenance": "live_agent_self_discovery"
